1 | devops | Scaffold services/iris as a sibling of services/hermes, mirroring its shape exactly: own go.mod, cmd/iris, Dockerfile, README, a deploy-iris.yml workflow copied from deploy-hermes.yml with paths and names swapped, a make deploy-iris target, and docs/operations/env-vars-iris.md following env-vars-hermes.md. READ FIRST docs/prd/pages/assistant-channel.md sections 1, 3.0 to 3.2, 3.3.8, 6 and 8, then services/hermes end to end, .github/workflows/deploy-hermes.yml, scripts/check-hermes-isolation.sh and the Makefile target check-hermes-isolation. Extend the isolation gate so services/iris is held to the same rule as hermes (must not import apps/api/internal) and rename or generalise the script and target so both services are covered, with the CI job updated; the contract section 8 requires this in the Wave 1 PR that names the service, and this is that PR. Cloud Run: same region and project as hermes, min and max instances pinned to 1 for the pilot with the reason in the workflow comment, its own service secret name per section 8 (document it, do not create it in GCP here). Do not write Go beyond a main that starts an HTTP server answering GET /healthz with the commit SHA the way the API does (see the deployment doc for the version contract). VERIFY: go build in services/iris, the isolation gate green for both services, make check-ci-wiring green, and paste real exit codes. HOUSE STYLE: no long dash anywhere, no AI tool or vendor name, no Co-Authored-By trailer. Open a draft PR with Refs 2800. Do not merge. | feat/ac-w2a-iris-scaffold

2 | go-backend | On branch feat/ac-w2a-iris-scaffold, inside services/iris only, add the edge of the connector, no tools yet. READ FIRST docs/prd/pages/assistant-channel.md sections 1, 3.0 to 3.2, 3.3.8, 3.4 and 6, then the W1-D authorization server on main (apps/api/internal/handler/assistant_oauth.go, service/assistant_auth.go, middleware/auth.go: the assistant scope, the binding_id claim, the per-binding liveness check), and the MCP specification 2025-11-25 sections on streamable HTTP transport, authorization, protected resource metadata and tool annotations, which you fetch and cite by section in code comments. Deliver: ONE, a streamable HTTP MCP endpoint that on a missing or invalid bearer answers 401 with a WWW-Authenticate header carrying resource_metadata, and serves /.well-known/oauth-protected-resource naming the API as the authorization server, exactly as both pilot hosts require per section 6. TWO, bearer validation: the connector verifies the assistant access token the same way the API middleware does, sharing no code with apps/api/internal (copy the minimal verification into services/iris or call the existing verification of the API endpoint if one exists; state which and why in the README), rejects any token without the assistant scope, and forwards the binding_id and user for every downstream call. THREE, a tool manifest that lists the seven Wave 2 tool names from section 3.3 with their titles, descriptions and annotations (readOnlyHint true for the six read tools, false for update_trip, destructiveHint false everywhere, openWorldHint false), returning not_available for the two Wave 3 names per section 3.3.8, and a stub handler per tool that returns not_available with the section 3.4 error shape; the real tools come in W2-B. FOUR, a per-host kill switch: an env var listing disabled client ids; a call from a disabled host answers not_available with a fixed sentence and is logged once per binding per hour. FIVE, structured logging per call with binding_id, client_id, tool name, outcome and duration, never any argument or result payload, following docs/operations/provider-refusal-logging.md. VERIFY: go test ./... in services/iris with the race flag, including tests for the 401 shape, the scope rejection, the manifest annotations and the kill switch; paste real exit codes. HOUSE STYLE: no long dash anywhere, no AI tool or vendor name in code comments, commit or PR text, no Co-Authored-By trailer. Push to the same branch, keep the PR a draft. | feat/ac-w2a-iris-scaffold

3 | backend-critic | READ-ONLY REVIEW of the draft PR on branch feat/ac-w2a-iris-scaffold. No commits, no push, no review, no labels, no edits; tmp writes only. Record head SHA at start and at report. THE QUESTIONS THAT MATTER: is the service a boundary and nothing else (grep for any supplier, payment or ranking import or logic: any hit is a BLOCK per contract section 1); does the isolation gate actually fail when services/iris imports apps/api/internal (prove it with a throwaway import in a tmp worktree and paste the red run); does the 401 carry the resource_metadata pointer both hosts need and does the protected resource metadata name the API issuer; does a token without the assistant scope get rejected before any downstream call; are the manifest annotations exactly what section 6 says the hosts key confirmation on; does the kill switch cut one host without touching the other. Write failing tests for anything that fails and paste real results. Post ONE comment on issue 2340 whose first line reads CRITIC W2A IRIS SCAFFOLD with SAFE-TO-MERGE or BLOCK-FIX and file and line citations. HOUSE STYLE: no long dash, no AI tool or vendor name. | feat/ac-w2a-iris-scaffold

4 | security-reviewer | SECURITY REVIEW of the draft PR on branch feat/ac-w2a-iris-scaffold, the new connector service. Read-only; tmp writes only. Record head SHA at start and at report. Threat model: a hostile host with a valid token for one traveller trying to reach another traveller or another scope; a token minted for the web session paths replayed against the connector; a revoked binding whose token is still within its lifetime; a host on the kill list retrying; discovery endpoints used to fingerprint or enumerate. For each, state whether the implementation defeats it with file and line, or write the exploit. Also check: the service holds no secret it does not need, the Cloud Run deploy pins instances and the reason is written, no log line carries a token, argument or result payload, the well-known responses set no-store, and the service token name in the env docs matches the workflow. Post ONE comment on issue 2340 whose first line reads SECURITY W2A IRIS SCAFFOLD with a severity per finding and a final SAFE or BLOCK line. HOUSE STYLE: no long dash, no AI tool or vendor name. | feat/ac-w2a-iris-scaffold
